## N₃: Parallelepipeds Through 4 Non-coplanar Points

The 4 beacons are affinely independent. A parallelepiped is an affine image of the unit cube {0,1}³. An affine map f is uniquely determined by the images of 4 affinely independent points.

**Counting affinely independent 4-subsets of cube vertices.** There are C(8,4) = 70 four-subsets. Coplanar ones: 6 faces + 6 diagonal rectangles (planes like x+y=1, x=y, etc.) = 12. So affinely independent 4-subsets = 70 − 12 = **58**.

**Counting parallelepipeds.** An ordered assignment of our 4 points to an ordered affinely independent 4-tuple of cube vertices determines a unique affine map f, hence a unique parallelepiped f({0,1}³). The number of ordered affinely independent 4-tuples is 58 × 4! = 58 × 24 = 1392.

Two ordered assignments yield the **same** parallelepiped Q iff they differ by an affine symmetry σ of the cube (the full octahedral group, |G| = 2³·3! = **48**): if f' = f∘σ⁻¹, then f'(v'_i) = P_i requires v'_i = σ(v_i). The action is **free**: a cube symmetry fixing 4 affinely independent vertices is the identity. Therefore each parallelepiped is represented by exactly 48 ordered assignments.

$$N_3 = \frac{1392}{48} = \frac{58 \times 24}{48} = \frac{58}{2} = 29.$$

**N₃ = 29.**

